  # round by design, so if it inherits a lease descriptor the lease outlives the round
  # too and every later attempt — including --adopt — is refused by its own subject.
  # Closing them here is what makes the lease mean "a round is running" again instead of
  # "a round once started here". fd 7 is the FIXTURE lease, taken later in GRADE; closing
  # a descriptor that is not open is not an error, so this is correct in every mode.
  #
  # `setsid --wait` (util-linux) is the whole supervision repair in one flag: the
  # wrapper still gets its own session — so a dead terminal cannot HUP the builder,
  # which is why setsid was here in the first place — and setsid then WAITS on it and
  # exits with the same status. So $! is a child we can `wait` on, and its status is
  # the builder's status. Plain `setsid` (no --wait) returns immediately and throws the
  # exit code away; that is the false GREEN.
  BUILDER_LAUNCHER=$LAUNCHER
